// Job Seeking Skills lessons 6–10: resume design and accuracy, preparing for the interview, first impressions,
// answering questions professionally, and closing with final preparation.
const RULES=['HOSA Job Seeking Skills 2026–27 guidelines and rating sheets','https://hosa.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Job-Seeking-26-27.pdf'];
const OWLRES=['Purdue OWL: Résumés and CVs','https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/job_search_writing/resumes_and_vitas/index.html'];
const COSINT=['CareerOneStop: Get ready to interview','https://www.careeronestop.org/JobSearch/Interview/get-ready.aspx'];
const SAFE=['CareerSafe','https://www.careersafeonline.com/'];
const UPLOAD=['HOSA: Competitive event digital uploads','https://hosa.org/competitive-event-digital-uploads/'];
const OWLLET=['Purdue OWL: Job search letters','https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/job_search_writing/job_search_letters/index.html'];
const q=(p,a,b,c,d,e)=>[p,[a,b,c,d],e];
const match=(title,pairs)=>({t:'w',kind:'match',title,pairs});
const order=(title,steps)=>({t:'w',kind:'order',title,steps});
const sort=(title,buckets,items)=>({t:'w',kind:'sort',title,buckets,items});
const flip=(title,cards)=>({t:'w',kind:'flip',title,cards});
const kase=(title,nodes)=>({t:'w',kind:'case',title,start:'a',nodes});
const chk=(q,options,why)=>({t:'check',q,options,why});
const ex=(title,prompt,steps,answer)=>({t:'example',title,prompt,steps,answer});

export const JS6={title:'Resume design, creativity and accuracy',hook:'Resume creativity is worth 20 points—and a messy or crowded page can undercut great content. Judges look for a resume that stands out professionally, fits on one page and contains no spelling or grammar errors. This lesson covers layout, visual design, proofreading and preparing the combined PDF.',objectives:['Design a clean, professional one-page resume.','Use creativity appropriately for a health care audience.','Proofread for zero errors and consistent formatting.','Combine the statement and resume into one PDF.'],sections:[
['Layout basics',[
'A strong layout uses **clear section headings**, **consistent alignment**, **readable fonts** (often 10–12 point for body text), **adequate margins** and **white space** so the eye can scan quickly. Recruiters often skim a resume in seconds, so the most important information should be easy to find.',
'Keep formatting consistent: the same style for every heading, the same date format and the same bullet style. Use bold for emphasis sparingly.',
"Section headings in slightly larger or bold text help judges navigate quickly. Keep them short, such as “Education,” “Experience” and “Skills.”",
"Use a consistent date format, such as “Sept. 2025–Present,” for every entry. Small consistencies make the whole resume look polished.",
"Leave enough space between lines and sections that the page does not feel crowded. A slightly shorter resume with clear spacing reads better than a cramped one.",
sort('Good layout practice?',['Good','Poor'],[['Consistent headings and alignment',0],['Five different fonts',1],['White space between sections',0],['Tiny 7-point text to fit more',1],['Dates aligned on the right margin',0],['Random bold and italic everywhere',1]])]],
['Creativity with professionalism',[
'The **creativity** item rewards a resume that incorporates creativity while remaining professional. Appropriate creativity includes a distinctive but readable **header with your name**, a subtle **color accent**, well-designed **section dividers**, a clean **two-column layout** or icons for contact details.',
'Avoid distractions: photos, clip art, bright backgrounds, decorative fonts and graphics that make reading harder. In health care, a polished, clean design signals professionalism.',
"Print the resume in black and white to check that it still looks clear. Some reviewers print documents, and color accents should not be necessary for readability.",
"Ask a teacher or career counselor whether the design looks professional. An outside opinion helps balance creativity with the expectations of health care employers.",
match('Design choice and verdict',[['Name in a larger, bold font','Appropriate creativity'],['Subtle navy accent color','Appropriate creativity'],['Clean two-column layout','Appropriate creativity'],['Neon background','Distracting'],['Cartoon clip art','Distracting'],['Script font for body text','Hard to read']])]],
['Fitting on one page',[
'The resume must **not exceed one page** (5 points). Prioritize relevant, recent and impressive items. Combine similar entries, shorten bullets, remove outdated middle school activities (for older students) and adjust margins slightly if needed—but never shrink text below a readable size.',
'If you have limited experience, a well-organized one-page resume with strong education, skills and volunteer sections is better than padding.',
"A focused one-page resume is easier for judges to read quickly. Every line should earn its place by showing relevant skills or accomplishments.",
"If you have more strong content than fits, keep the most recent and relevant items and save the rest for other applications or the interview.",
ex('Trimming to one page','Your resume spills 6 lines onto page two (scenario invented for practice). Fix it.',['Remove a less relevant activity','Combine two similar volunteer roles into one entry','Shorten three long bullets to one line each','Reduce spacing between sections slightly','Check that text remains at least 10 points'],'One clean page with the strongest content (scenario invented for practice).')]],
['Proofreading for zero errors',[
'Spelling and grammar earn 5 points only with **zero errors**. Check names, dates, organizations, certifications and medical terms. Make tense consistent (past for completed roles, present for current), punctuation consistent and capitalization correct.',
'Read the resume backward line by line to catch typos, print it to check visually and ask at least two people to review it.',
"Check that every bullet starts with the same kind of word—usually an action verb—and that punctuation at the ends of bullets is consistent.",
"Check that your email address and phone number are correct. A typo in contact information could prevent an employer from reaching you in a real application.",
order('Resume proofreading checklist',['Run spell-check and grammar check','Verify names, dates and titles','Check consistent tense and punctuation','Read backward line by line','Print and review visually','Ask two people to review'])]],
['Accuracy and honesty',[
'The guidelines require the resume and statement to be **factual and accurate**, including **real** work experience, education and accomplishments. Never exaggerate titles, invent experiences or inflate numbers. Interview questions may ask about anything on your resume, so you must be able to explain each item.',
'Honesty is also a core health care value—employers trust candidates whose claims hold up.',
"Before the interview, review each resume item and prepare a brief explanation. Judges may point to a line and ask, “Tell us more about this.”",
"Consistency between documents matters. If your resume lists a job title, use the same title when you mention it in the personal statement.",
chk('You volunteered 30 hours. What should your resume say?',['30 hours (or “30+” only if more)','100 hours to look stronger','Leave out the hours and claim leadership','Say you were the director'],'Accuracy is required.')]],
['Creating the combined PDF',[
'Combine the **title page**, **personal statement** (up to two pages) and **resume** into **one PDF** and upload by **May 15 at midnight EST**. If it is not a single PDF, judges will not view it, and inaccessible links earn no points.',
'Export each document to PDF to preserve formatting, then merge them in order. Open the final file to check fonts, page breaks and headers. If your state used the upload system, upload again for ILC.',
"Name the file clearly, such as with your last name and event, and keep a copy on your device and in cloud storage in case you need to resubmit.",
order('Building the upload',['Finalize the title page','Finalize the personal statement','Finalize the resume','Export each to PDF','Merge into one PDF in order','Open and check every page','Upload by May 15'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Design and upload',{a:{text:'Your resume uses a bright pink background and cartoon stethoscope clip art. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you change?',choices:[['Use a clean background with a subtle accent color and no clip art','b','Correct.',true],['Add more clip art','b','Creativity must stay professional.',false]]},b:{text:'You upload the statement and resume as two separate files. What happens?',choices:[['Judges will not view it—upload one combined PDF','c','Correct.',true],['Judges will merge them','c','Only one PDF is accepted.',false]]},c:{text:'Your polished, single-PDF submission is ready.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Use clear headings, consistent alignment, readable fonts, margins and white space; creativity (20 points) should be distinctive yet professional—bold name header, subtle color, clean layout—without photos, clip art or loud backgrounds.','Keep the resume to one page (5 points) by prioritizing relevant content without shrinking text; proofread for zero errors (5 points) and keep all details factual and explainable.','Combine the title page, personal statement and resume into one PDF, check every page and upload by May 15 at midnight EST.'],terms:[['White space','Empty space that makes a document easier to read.'],['Combined PDF','A single file containing all required documents in order.'],['Consistency','Using the same formatting choices throughout a document.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is resume creativity worth?','20','5','10','15','Rubric.'),q('Which is appropriate creativity?','A subtle accent color','Cartoon clip art','A neon background','A selfie','Professional.'),q('What is the maximum resume length?','One page','Two pages','Three pages','Unlimited','Rule.'),q('Spelling and grammar earn full points with:','Zero errors','Three errors','Five errors','Any number','Rubric.'),q('How must documents be uploaded?','As one combined PDF','As separate files','As images','As a link','Rule.'),q('Which proofreading technique catches typos?','Reading backward line by line','Skimming once','Only using spell-check','Not reading','Technique.'),q('If you volunteered 30 hours, you should list:','30 hours','100 hours','No hours and claim more','500 hours','Accuracy.'),q('What should you avoid to fit on one page?','Shrinking text below a readable size','Trimming weak entries','Combining similar roles','Shortening bullets','Readability.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would design, proofread and submit a resume and personal statement that earn full points.',rubric:[['Design',['headings','alignment','fonts','white space','creativity','professional']],['Length and accuracy',['one page','prioritize','factual','accurate','explain']],['Proofreading',['zero errors','tense','names','backward','review']],['Upload',['title page','statement','resume','one pdf','check','may 15']]],model:'I would design my resume with clear section headings, consistent left alignment, an 11-point readable font, 0.75- to 1-inch margins and white space between sections so judges can scan it quickly. For creativity, I would use my name in a larger bold font as a header, a subtle navy accent color for headings and thin section dividers, avoiding photos, clip art and bright backgrounds that would look unprofessional in health care. To keep it to one page, I would prioritize my CNA clinical rotation, CPR certification and senior center volunteering, combine similar entries, shorten bullets to one line and remove less relevant activities, without shrinking text below 10 points. I would make sure every detail is factual and accurate—listing my real 120 volunteer hours, not an inflated number—because judges may ask about anything on my resume. To proofread, I would run spell-check, verify names, dates and certification titles, check consistent past tense for completed roles and present tense for current ones, read the resume backward line by line, print it and ask my advisor and a teacher to review it so it has zero errors. Finally, I would export my title page, two-page personal statement and resume as PDFs, merge them in order into one combined PDF, open it to check fonts, headers and page breaks and upload it well before May 15 at midnight EST, uploading again for ILC if my state used the system.'},sources:[RULES,OWLRES,UPLOAD]};

export const JS7={title:'Preparing for the interview',hook:'Judges notice preparation immediately: competitors who know the position, have practiced their answers and can give clear examples score far higher than those who improvise. With no materials allowed in the interview, everything you need must be in your head. This lesson builds your preparation plan, starting with the first two questions you know will be asked.',objectives:['Prepare answers to the two guaranteed questions.','Anticipate common interview questions.','Use the STAR method to structure examples.','Practice effectively without memorizing scripts.'],sections:[
['The two guaranteed questions',[
'The first two questions always ask you to **explain the job, internship or opportunity** you are interviewing for and **the skills you would bring** to it. Prepare clear, confident answers of about 30–45 seconds each.',
'For the first, describe the position, its main responsibilities and why it interests you. For the second, name **three key skills** that match the position, each with a brief example.',
"Practice these two answers until they feel natural. A confident start sets the tone for the rest of the interview and calms nerves.",
"Avoid simply listing skills. Each skill should come with a short proof, such as a specific experience or result, to make it believable.",
ex('Answering the first two questions','Outline answers for a CNA position (details invented for practice).',['Q1: “I’m interviewing for a Certified Nursing Assistant position at Oakwood Senior Living, helping residents with daily living, measuring vital signs and reporting changes to nurses.”','Why: “I want to support residents’ dignity and independence.”','Q2 skill 1: Compassion—weekly senior center volunteering','Q2 skill 2: Accuracy—measuring vitals during clinicals','Q2 skill 3: Reliability—perfect attendance at my job'],'Clear, specific answers that open the interview strongly (details invented for practice).')]],
['Common interview questions',[
'Expect questions such as: **Tell us about yourself.** **Why do you want this position?** **What are your strengths and weaknesses?** **Describe a time you worked on a team.** **Tell us about a challenge you overcame.** **How do you handle stress?** **Where do you see yourself in five years?** **Why should we choose you?** **Do you have any questions for us?**',
'HOSA provides sample secret questions; practice with them and similar questions. Interview questions are secret during competition, so do not share the actual questions afterward.',
"For “Tell us about yourself,” use a simple present-past-future structure: who you are now, relevant experiences that brought you here and why you want this opportunity.",
"Group questions into themes—motivation, teamwork, challenges, goals—and prepare one or two stories for each theme rather than a separate answer for every possible question.",
match('Question and what judges want to learn',[['Tell us about yourself','A focused professional summary'],['Strengths and weaknesses','Self-awareness and growth'],['Teamwork example','Collaboration skills'],['Challenge overcome','Resilience and problem solving'],['Handling stress','Coping and composure'],['Five-year goal','Direction and commitment']])]],
['The STAR method',[
'Behavioral questions (“Tell us about a time…”) are best answered with **STAR**: **Situation** (brief context), **Task** (your responsibility), **Action** (what you did—the most important part) and **Result** (the outcome and what you learned). STAR keeps answers organized, specific and concise.',
'Prepare five or six STAR stories from your inventory that can answer many questions: teamwork, leadership, conflict, a mistake, helping someone and a challenge.',
"One STAR story can answer several questions. A story about organizing a blood drive might show leadership, teamwork or problem solving depending on how you frame it.",
"Keep the situation and task brief—one or two sentences—so most of the answer focuses on what you did and the result.",
order('STAR answer structure',['Situation','Task','Action','Result'])]],
['Strengths and weaknesses',[
'For **strengths**, choose qualities relevant to the position and back them with evidence. For **weaknesses**, choose a real but manageable area and explain **how you are improving**: “I used to hesitate to ask questions, so during clinicals I set a goal to ask my supervising nurse one question every shift.”',
'Avoid clichés such as “I’m a perfectionist” without explanation, and never name a weakness that is essential to the job, such as being careless with details for a pharmacy role.',
"Strengths are most convincing when backed by specific evidence. Choose strengths you can prove with stories from your inventory.",
"When describing strengths, avoid listing too many. Two or three well-supported strengths are more memorable than a long list.",
sort('Strong or weak answer about weaknesses?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Naming a real area and describing steps to improve',0],['“I have no weaknesses.”',1],['“I used to procrastinate, so I now use a planner and set early deadlines.”',0],['“I’m careless with medications.” (for a pharmacy role)',1],['“I’m a perfectionist.” with no explanation',1]])]],
['Questions to ask the interviewer',[
'Interviews often end with, “Do you have any questions for us?” Prepare one or two thoughtful questions that show interest: “What qualities do your most successful CNAs share?” or “How does your team support new employees during training?”',
'Avoid questions answered by basic research or focused only on pay and time off early in the process.',
"Prepare a backup question in case your first one is answered during the interview, so you are never caught without something thoughtful to ask.",
"Write down your questions for interviewers ahead of time and memorize them, since you cannot bring notes into the interview.",
chk('Which is the best question to ask interviewers?',['“What qualities do your most successful team members share?”','“How much vacation do I get?”','“What does your company do?”','“Can I leave early on Fridays?”'],'Shows genuine interest.')]],
['Practicing effectively',[
'Practice answers **aloud**, but do not memorize scripts word for word—memorized answers sound robotic and fall apart under pressure. Instead, memorize **key points and examples** and practice expressing them naturally.',
'Hold **mock interviews** with advisors, teachers, family or career counselors, ideally in business attire. Record practice sessions to review eye contact, posture and filler words, and practice staying within the five-minute limit.',
"Practice in the clothes and shoes you will wear. Feeling comfortable in professional attire reduces distractions on interview day.",
"Practice with someone who asks unexpected follow-up questions. Learning to adapt your prepared stories builds the flexibility real interviews require.",
order('Interview preparation plan',['Research the position','Prepare answers to the two guaranteed questions','Build five or six STAR stories','Practice common questions aloud','Hold mock interviews in business attire','Review recordings and refine'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Preparation choices',{a:{text:'You memorize every answer word for word and freeze when a question is worded differently. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is a better approach?',choices:[['Memorize key points and STAR stories, then practice speaking naturally','b','Correct.',true],['Memorize even more scripts','b','Flexibility beats scripts.',false]]},b:{text:'A judge asks, “Tell us about a time you made a mistake.” How do you answer?',choices:[['Use STAR: brief situation, your responsibility, what you did to fix it and what you learned','c','Correct.',true],['Say you never make mistakes','c','Honesty and growth are valued.',false]]},c:{text:'You answer confidently with a clear STAR story.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['The first two questions always ask you to explain the opportunity and the skills you bring—prepare 30–45-second answers naming three skills with examples.','Anticipate common questions (tell us about yourself, why this position, strengths and weaknesses, teamwork, challenges, stress, goals) and answer behavioral questions with STAR (situation, task, action, result).','Present weaknesses honestly with improvement steps, prepare thoughtful questions for interviewers and practice key points aloud in mock interviews rather than memorizing scripts; keep questions confidential.'],terms:[['STAR method','Situation, task, action, result—a structure for answering behavioral questions.'],['Behavioral question','A question asking how you handled a past situation.'],['Mock interview','A practice interview that simulates the real experience.']],mcqs:[q('What do the first two questions always ask?','The opportunity and the skills you bring','Your hobbies','Your favorite class','Other competitors','Rule.'),q('In STAR, the A stands for:','Action','Answer','Attitude','Award','Method.'),q('Which part of STAR is usually most important?','Action','Situation','Task','Title','Method.'),q('Which weakness answer is strongest?','A real area plus steps you are taking to improve','“I have no weaknesses.”','A weakness essential to the job','“I’m perfect.”','Self-awareness.'),q('Which is a good question to ask interviewers?','“What qualities do your most successful employees share?”','“How much vacation do I get?”','“What do you do here?”','“Can I leave early?”','Interest.'),q('How should you practice answers?','Practice key points aloud naturally','Memorize scripts word for word','Never practice','Only think about them silently','Practice.'),q('Behavioral questions often begin with:','“Tell us about a time…”','“What is 2 + 2?”','“Spell your name.”','“What color is the sky?”','Question type.'),q('Interview questions should be:','Kept confidential until the event ends','Shared online','Given to friends competing later','Posted on social media','Ethics.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would prepare for the Job Seeking Skills interview, including the guaranteed questions and behavioral questions.',rubric:[['Guaranteed questions',['first two','opportunity','skills','three','examples','30']],['Common questions',['tell us about yourself','strengths','weaknesses','teamwork','challenge','questions for us']],['STAR',['star','situation','task','action','result','stories']],['Practice',['aloud','key points','not memorize','mock interviews','record','five minutes']]],model:'I would prepare first for the two guaranteed questions. For the first, I would explain in about 40 seconds that I am interviewing for a CNA position at Oakwood Senior Living, where CNAs help residents with daily living, measure vital signs and report changes to nurses, and that I want to support residents’ dignity and independence. For the second, I would name three skills—compassion, accuracy and reliability—each supported by a brief example from my senior center volunteering, clinical rotation and part-time job. Next, I would anticipate common questions such as tell us about yourself, why this position, strengths and weaknesses, teamwork, handling stress and five-year goals. For behavioral questions, I would prepare six STAR stories describing the situation, my task, my actions and the result—for example, the time I noticed a resident was confused and reported it to the nurse, who found an infection early. For weaknesses, I would name a real area, such as hesitating to ask questions, and explain my plan of asking my supervising nurse one question every shift. I would prepare one thoughtful question for the judges, such as what qualities their most successful CNAs share. Finally, I would practice key points aloud instead of memorizing scripts, hold weekly mock interviews in business attire with my advisor and family, record them to check eye contact and filler words and time myself to stay within five minutes, keeping actual questions confidential.'},sources:[RULES,COSINT]};

export const JS8={title:'First impressions and nonverbal communication',hook:'Judges begin forming impressions the moment you walk in. The introduction and first impression item is worth 15 points, and eye contact, poise and posture add 10 more. Your greeting, handshake, attire, posture and expression communicate confidence and professionalism before you answer a single question.',objectives:['Make a strong, professional first impression.','Use an appropriate greeting and handshake.','Demonstrate confident body language and eye contact.','Dress professionally for the interview.'],sections:[
['The first impression',[
'The top score describes an **excellent greeting**, a **correct handshake** and **professional engagement** with the judges upon arrival—a “great first impression.” Lower scores describe greetings that do not stand out, missing or incorrect handshakes and conversation that is not engaging.',
'When introduced, walk in confidently, smile, make eye contact, greet the judges (“Good morning, it’s nice to meet you”), offer a handshake and wait to be invited to sit.',
"Smile genuinely and use the judges’ names if you hear them during introductions. Small courtesies help build an immediate connection.",
"Your first impression begins before you enter the room. Be polite to staff and other competitors in the waiting area; professionalism should be consistent.",
order('Arriving at the interview',['Wait calmly to be introduced','Enter with good posture and a smile','Make eye contact and greet the judges','Offer a firm handshake','Wait to be invited to sit','Sit up straight, ready to begin'])]],
['The professional handshake',[
'A professional handshake is **firm but not crushing**, uses the **right hand**, connects **palm to palm** with the web of your thumb meeting theirs, lasts about **two to three seconds** with one or two gentle pumps and comes with **eye contact** and a smile. Handshakes are allowed and encouraged at both the beginning and end in this event.',
'Avoid limp “dead fish” handshakes, finger-only grips, overly long shakes or looking away. If you have sweaty palms, discreetly wipe them beforehand.',
"If a judge does not extend a hand, follow their lead and greet them warmly instead. Courtesy and adaptability matter more than the handshake itself.",
"Practice handshakes with family members or classmates and ask for honest feedback on firmness and timing. Small adjustments make a big difference.",
sort('Good handshake practice?',['Good','Poor'],[['Firm grip, palm to palm, with eye contact',0],['Limp grip with fingers only',1],['Two or three seconds with a smile',0],['Crushing the judge’s hand',1],['Looking at the floor while shaking',1]])]],
['Body language',[
'**Posture:** sit up straight with feet flat, shoulders relaxed and leaning slightly forward to show interest. **Hands:** rest them in your lap or on the table and use natural gestures; avoid fidgeting, tapping or touching your face or hair. **Facial expression:** smile naturally and show engagement; nod when listening.',
'Body language should match your words—confident answers delivered with slumped posture lose impact.',
"Match your expression to the content: a warm smile when describing why you love helping others and a serious, focused look when discussing safety or responsibility.",
"Practice in front of a mirror or recording to spot habits you may not notice, such as touching your face or rocking in your chair.",
match('Nonverbal cue and message',[['Sitting up straight','Confidence and attention'],['Leaning slightly forward','Interest'],['Nodding while listening','Engagement'],['Crossed arms','Defensiveness'],['Fidgeting','Nervousness'],['Natural smile','Warmth and approachability']])]],
['Eye contact',[
'Maintain **natural eye contact** with each judge—look at the person asking a question while listening, and share eye contact among all judges while answering. Brief glances away to think are normal; avoid staring or looking only at the floor or ceiling.',
'A helpful technique is to finish each sentence or key point while looking at one judge, then move to another.',
"Eye contact also shows honesty and confidence. If direct eye contact is uncomfortable, looking at the bridge of the nose or between the eyes can appear natural.",
"Keep your voice steady and warm. A confident tone works together with eye contact and posture to create a strong overall impression.",
chk('With three judges, where should you look while answering?',['Share eye contact among all three, especially the one who asked','Only at the floor','Only at one judge the entire time','At the ceiling'],'Engage the panel.')]],
['Professional attire',[
'Dress in **business attire** or the **official HOSA uniform**—bonus points are awarded for proper dress. Business attire generally means a suit or blazer with dress pants or skirt, a collared shirt or blouse and dress shoes. Clothing should be **clean, pressed and well-fitting**.',
'Grooming matters: neat hair, clean nails, minimal fragrance and simple accessories. In health care settings, professionals often limit jewelry and strong scents for safety and patient comfort.',
"Try on your outfit before competition day to check fit and comfort, and prepare it the night before to avoid last-minute stress.",
"Choose comfortable, professional shoes. Walking confidently is easier when you are not distracted by footwear that pinches or slips.",
sort('Appropriate for the interview?',['Appropriate','Not appropriate'],[['Pressed blazer and dress pants',0],['Ripped jeans',1],['Official HOSA uniform',0],['Strong cologne',1],['Clean, polished dress shoes',0],['Flip-flops',1]])]],
['Managing nerves',[
'Nervousness is normal. Before entering, take slow breaths, relax your shoulders and remind yourself of your preparation. Arrive early to avoid rushing. If your voice shakes at first, slow down—most nerves fade after the first question.',
'Positive self-talk (“I’ve practiced this; I’m ready”) and power poses before entering can boost confidence. If you lose your train of thought, pause, take a breath and continue.',
"Remember that judges want you to succeed. Viewing the interview as a professional conversation rather than a test can ease anxiety.",
"If you feel nervous, focus on one friendly face among the judges at first, then expand eye contact to the others as you settle in.",
ex('A calming routine','Plan a two-minute routine before your interview.',['Breathe in for 4 counts and out for 6, five times','Roll shoulders back and stand tall','Review your three key skills mentally','Smile and repeat, “I’m prepared and ready.”'],'A routine that steadies nerves before entering.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('First impressions',{a:{text:'You enter the room looking down at your shoes and say nothing. (Scenario invented for practice.) What would make a better first impression?',choices:[['Look up, smile, greet the judges and offer a firm handshake','b','Correct.',true],['Sit down immediately without speaking','b','Greeting and handshake are scored.',false]]},b:{text:'During the interview you notice you are tapping your pen. What do you do?',choices:[['Set your hands calmly in your lap and continue','c','Correct.',true],['Tap faster','c','Fidgeting signals nervousness.',false]]},c:{text:'Your body language now supports your confident answers.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Introduction and first impression is worth 15 points: enter confidently, smile, make eye contact, greet the judges, shake hands correctly and wait to be seated.','A professional handshake is firm, palm to palm, two to three seconds with eye contact; body language includes upright posture, leaning slightly forward, calm hands, natural smiles and nods; eye contact, poise and posture is worth 10 points.','Wear business attire or the HOSA uniform (bonus points) with neat grooming and minimal fragrance; manage nerves with breathing, early arrival and positive self-talk.'],terms:[['First impression','The initial judgment people form when meeting you.'],['Nonverbal communication','Messages sent through posture, expression, gestures and eye contact.'],['Poise','Calm, confident composure.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is the introduction and first impression worth?','15','5','10','20','Rubric.'),q('A professional handshake is:','Firm, palm to palm, with eye contact','Limp and brief','Crushing','Finger-only','Handshake.'),q('When are handshakes allowed in this event?','At the beginning and end','Never','Only at the end','Only if judges ask twice','Rule.'),q('Which posture shows interest?','Sitting up and leaning slightly forward','Slouching back','Crossing arms','Turning sideways','Body language.'),q('Crossed arms may signal:','Defensiveness','Enthusiasm','Agreement','Confidence','Nonverbal.'),q('Which attire earns bonus points?','Business attire or official HOSA uniform','Gym clothes','Pajamas','Swimsuits','Dress code.'),q('With three judges, you should:','Share eye contact among all of them','Look only at the floor','Stare at one judge','Close your eyes','Eye contact.'),q('If you lose your train of thought, you should:','Pause, breathe and continue','Leave the room','Laugh nervously for a minute','Give up','Composure.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would make a strong first impression and use nonverbal communication effectively in your interview.',rubric:[['Arrival and greeting',['smile','eye contact','greet','handshake','wait to sit']],['Handshake and body language',['firm','palm','posture','lean','hands','fidget','nod']],['Eye contact and attire',['eye contact','judges','business attire','hosa uniform','grooming']],['Nerves',['breathe','early','self-talk','pause','calm']]],model:'I would arrive early, wearing a pressed navy blazer, dress pants, a collared shirt and polished shoes, with neat hair and no strong fragrance, to earn the dress bonus. Before my turn, I would take five slow breaths, roll my shoulders back and remind myself of my three key skills. When the section leader introduced me, I would walk in with good posture, smile, make eye contact and say, “Good morning, it’s nice to meet you,” offering each judge a firm, palm-to-palm handshake for two or three seconds while looking them in the eye. I would wait to be invited to sit, then sit up straight with my feet flat, hands resting calmly in my lap and leaning slightly forward to show interest. While a judge asked a question, I would look at that judge and nod to show I was listening, and while answering, I would share eye contact among all the judges, finishing key points while looking at one before moving to another. I would use natural gestures to emphasize points and avoid fidgeting, tapping or touching my hair. If I felt nervous or lost my place, I would pause, take a breath and continue calmly. At the end, I would stand, thank each judge and offer another handshake, leaving the same confident impression I made on arrival—important because the first impression is worth 15 points and eye contact, poise and posture another 10.'},sources:[RULES,SAFE]};

export const JS9={title:'Answering questions professionally',hook:'Judges score your answers on four criteria: you volunteer information, respond appropriately to every question, relate your strengths and skills to the job and sound professional. Meeting all four, with ease and conviction, earns the top 15 points—and leaves judges excited to know more about you. Your knowledge of the position earns 10 more.',objectives:['Meet all four interview answer criteria.','Connect every answer to the position.','Use professional vocabulary and concise structure.','Handle difficult or unexpected questions.'],sections:[
['The four answer criteria',[
'Judges look for answers in which you **(a) volunteer information**—go beyond yes or no with relevant details; **(b) respond appropriately to every question**—answer what was asked; **(c) relate strengths and skills to the job**; and **(d) sound professional** in vocabulary and descriptions.',
'The top score showcases all four with ease and conviction; covering three, two, one or none earns progressively fewer points.',
"Answering appropriately also means matching the tone of the question—serious for questions about mistakes or safety, warm and personal for questions about motivation.",
"Practice each criterion separately at first. For example, answer several questions focusing only on relating strengths to the job, then combine all four.",
flip('Interview answer criteria',[['Volunteer information','Share relevant details beyond yes or no'],['Respond appropriately','Answer the actual question'],['Relate strengths','Connect skills to the job'],['Sound professional','Use professional vocabulary'],['Top score','All four with ease and conviction'],['Points','15']])]],
['Volunteering information',[
'Avoid one-word or very short answers. When asked, “Do you work well on teams?” do not just say “Yes.” Give a brief example: “Yes—during my clinical rotation, I worked with two classmates to complete morning care for six residents, and we divided tasks based on each resident’s needs.”',
'At the same time, stay focused: aim for answers of about **30–60 seconds**, with one strong example rather than several rambling ones. The five-minute limit means every answer must count.',
"If you realize you are rambling, wrap up with a summary sentence that connects back to the question. It shows self-awareness and keeps answers focused.",
"Listen for the key word in each question—strength, challenge, teamwork—and make sure your example clearly demonstrates that quality.",
sort('Volunteers information effectively?',['Effective','Ineffective'],[['“Yes—for example, I organized our HOSA blood drive with three officers.”',0],['“Yes.”',1],['A focused 45-second example',0],['A 3-minute story about an unrelated vacation',1],['“I’m not sure.” with no attempt to explain',1]])]],
['Relating strengths to the job',[
'Connect every answer back to the position. After describing an experience, add a bridge sentence: “That experience taught me to stay calm under pressure, which is essential for a CNA when a resident’s condition changes.”',
'Use your match table: for each key requirement, have a story ready that proves you can meet it.',
"Prepare one bridge sentence for each key requirement in the job description. These short phrases make it easy to connect any story back to the position.",
"Use the language of the job description in your answers when appropriate. Hearing key words connected to your experiences helps judges see a clear fit.",
ex('Adding a bridge to the job','Connect an answer to a pharmacy technician position (details invented for practice).',['Story: double-checked cash register totals at a grocery job','Skill shown: attention to detail','Bridge: “As a pharmacy technician, that same habit of double-checking will help prevent medication errors.”'],'The bridge links past experience to the job (details invented for practice).')]],
['Professional vocabulary',[
'Use **professional, specific language** and correct health care terms: “residents,” “vital signs,” “infection control,” “patient privacy,” “interdisciplinary team.” Avoid slang (“like,” “kinda,” “super cool”), filler words and vague phrases.',
'Diction, pronunciation and grammar are scored separately (10 points). Speak clearly, use complete sentences and pronounce terms correctly.',
"Record yourself answering practice questions and count filler words. Replacing them with brief pauses makes you sound more confident and professional.",
"Pronounce health care terms correctly by practicing them aloud beforehand, especially longer words such as “interdisciplinary” or “confidentiality.”",
match('Casual phrase and professional alternative',[['“Stuff like that”','“Similar responsibilities”'],['“Kinda good at it”','“Skilled in”'],['“Old people”','“Older adults” or “residents”'],['“Taking their blood pressure thing”','“Measuring blood pressure”'],['“Super into helping”','“Committed to supporting”'],['“The doctors and stuff”','“The interdisciplinary team”']])]],
['Knowledge of the position',[
'The **knowledge of the position** item (10 points) rewards competitors who know the position, relate skills to it, have practiced questions and are prepared with research. Mention specific duties, the setting, the team you would work with and the organization’s mission.',
'If asked why you want the position, combine personal motivation with knowledge: “I want to work at Oakwood because its mission of person-centered care matches how I want to treat residents, and I know CNAs there support residents with daily living and report changes to nurses.”',
"Show knowledge naturally in answers rather than reciting facts. Mentioning the facility’s mission while explaining why you want the role sounds genuine.",
"Prepare a short answer explaining how the role fits into the larger health care team. Understanding the team shows mature knowledge of the position.",
chk('Which answer best shows knowledge of the position?',['Describing specific duties, the care team and the organization’s mission','“It seems fun.”','“I need money.”','“I don’t really know what it involves.”'],'Research shows.')]],
['Handling difficult questions',[
'For unexpected questions, **pause briefly** to think—a short pause is better than rambling. If a question is unclear, politely ask for clarification: “Could you clarify whether you mean school or work experience?” For questions about mistakes or conflicts, be honest, focus on what you learned and stay positive about others.',
'Never criticize former employers, teachers or teammates. If you lack direct experience, explain related experience and your willingness to learn.',
"Stay positive even when discussing challenges. Framing difficulties as learning experiences shows maturity and resilience.",
"If you make a mistake in an answer, it is acceptable to briefly correct yourself: “Let me clarify—I meant…” Calm corrections show composure.",
order('Responding to a tough question',['Listen to the full question','Pause briefly to think','Ask for clarification if needed','Answer honestly with a focused example','Explain what you learned','Connect back to the position'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Answer quality',{a:{text:'A judge asks, “Why should we choose you?” You answer, “Because I really want it.” (Scenario invented for practice.) How do you improve?',choices:[['Name two or three strengths with evidence and connect them to the position','b','Correct.',true],['Repeat that you really want it','b','Relate strengths to the job.',false]]},b:{text:'A judge asks about something you have never done. What do you say?',choices:[['Explain related experience and your eagerness to learn it','c','Correct.',true],['Pretend you have done it','c','Honesty matters.',false]]},c:{text:'Your answers now meet all four criteria.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['The 15-point answer item rewards volunteering information, responding appropriately, relating strengths and skills to the job and sounding professional—all four with ease and conviction.','Give focused 30–60-second answers with one strong example and a bridge sentence to the job; use professional vocabulary and correct terms (diction is scored separately).','Knowledge of the position (10 points) comes from research on duties, team and mission; handle tough questions by pausing, clarifying, answering honestly, staying positive and connecting back to the job.'],terms:[['Bridge sentence','A sentence connecting an example to the job’s requirements.'],['Professional vocabulary','Clear, specific language appropriate to the workplace.'],['Clarification','Asking for more detail to understand a question.']],mcqs:[q('Which is one of the four answer criteria?','Volunteer information','Tell jokes','Speak as fast as possible','Avoid eye contact','Rubric.'),q('How long should most answers be?','About 30–60 seconds','About 5 seconds','About 4 minutes','About 10 minutes','Focus.'),q('What does a bridge sentence do?','Connects an example to the job','Ends the interview','Changes the subject','Repeats the question','Technique.'),q('Which phrase is most professional?','“Older adults” or “residents”','“Old people”','“Those folks”','“Grandpa types”','Vocabulary.'),q('How many points is knowledge of the position worth?','10','5','15','20','Rubric.'),q('If a question is unclear, you should:','Politely ask for clarification','Guess randomly','Stay silent','Leave','Strategy.'),q('When discussing past conflicts, you should:','Stay positive and focus on what you learned','Criticize others','Blame teammates','Refuse to answer','Professionalism.'),q('If you lack direct experience, you should:','Explain related experience and willingness to learn','Pretend you have it','Say nothing','End the interview','Honesty.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would answer interview questions to meet all four criteria and show knowledge of the position.',rubric:[['Four criteria',['volunteer','respond','relate','strengths','professional']],['Answer structure',['example','30','60','focused','bridge','star']],['Vocabulary and knowledge',['professional','terms','duties','mission','team','research']],['Difficult questions',['pause','clarify','honest','positive','learn','connect']]],model:'To meet all four answer criteria, I would volunteer information by never answering with just yes or no—if asked whether I work well on teams, I would say yes and briefly describe dividing morning care tasks with two classmates for six residents during my clinical rotation. I would respond appropriately by listening to the full question and answering what was asked, keeping answers to about 30 to 60 seconds with one focused example, often in STAR form. I would relate my strengths to the job with bridge sentences, such as “That experience taught me to stay calm when a resident’s condition changes, which is essential for a CNA.” I would sound professional by using precise terms—residents, vital signs, infection control, the interdisciplinary team—avoiding slang and filler words and speaking in complete, clearly pronounced sentences. To show knowledge of the position, I would mention specific CNA duties at Oakwood, how CNAs work with nurses and Oakwood’s mission of person-centered care. If a question surprised me, I would pause briefly, ask for clarification if needed and answer honestly; for a question about a mistake, I would explain what happened, how I fixed it and what I learned, never criticizing others. If asked about a skill I had not yet used, I would describe related experience and my eagerness to learn. Delivered with ease and conviction, these answers would leave judges wanting to know more.'},sources:[RULES,COSINT]};

export const JS10={title:'Closing strong and final preparation',hook:'The last minute of an interview can be as memorable as the first. The closing is worth 10 points, and judges want to be left with a strong, positive overall impression. This final lesson covers closing statements, confidence and enthusiasm, the final checklist for your upload and interview day and how to keep improving.',objectives:['Deliver a strong, memorable closing.','Project confidence, maturity and enthusiasm.','Complete a final checklist for submission and interview day.','Reflect and continue developing job-seeking skills.'],sections:[
['The closing',[
'A strong **closing** leaves judges with a positive overall impression and the “wow factor.” When judges ask if you have anything to add or any questions, briefly **summarize why you are a strong fit**, express **genuine enthusiasm**, ask a thoughtful question if appropriate and **thank them**.',
'End with a confident handshake and eye contact—handshakes are encouraged at the end too.',
"If time runs short, a single strong summary sentence and a sincere thank-you are enough. A brief, confident close is better than a rushed, long one.",
"Practice your closing until it feels natural. Many competitors prepare strong openings but rush the ending, losing an easy chance to impress.",
ex('A closing statement','Draft a 20-second closing (details invented for practice).',['Summary: “My clinical training, my experience with older adults and my reliability prepare me well for this CNA role.”','Enthusiasm: “I’m excited about Oakwood’s person-centered care.”','Thanks: “Thank you for your time today.”','Handshake with eye contact'],'A concise, confident, memorable close (details invented for practice).')]],
['Confidence, maturity and enthusiasm',[
'Judges score **confidence, maturity and enthusiasm** (10 points): genuine excitement for the opportunity, mature conduct and confidence throughout—“they would make a great employee!” Show enthusiasm through your voice, expression and specific reasons for wanting the position.',
'Confidence comes from preparation. Maturity shows in how you handle tough questions, speak respectfully about others and stay composed if you stumble.',
"Enthusiasm should be genuine and specific. Saying exactly what excites you about the opportunity is more convincing than general statements of interest.",
"Let your enthusiasm show in your voice and expression, not only your words. Judges notice genuine energy and engagement.",
"Maturity also includes accepting feedback gracefully. If judges or advisors offer suggestions, thank them and use the advice to improve.",
sort('Shows confidence and maturity?',['Shows it','Undermines it'],[['Speaking calmly and clearly',0],['Apologizing repeatedly for being nervous',1],['Discussing a mistake honestly and what you learned',0],['Blaming a teacher for a low grade',1],['Sharing specific reasons you want the role',0]])]],
['Following up',[
'In real job searches, send a brief **thank-you note or email** within 24 hours, thanking interviewers, restating interest and mentioning something specific from the conversation. Although not required in HOSA competition, this habit sets you apart in real applications.',
'Keep your resume and statement updated as you gain experience, and save versions tailored to different opportunities.',
"Keep a list of people who helped you prepare and thank them after the event. Gratitude strengthens relationships that may help you in future applications.",
"Keep copies of your final documents and notes from your preparation. They form a strong starting point for future job, scholarship and college applications.",
chk('When should a thank-you note usually be sent after a real interview?',['Within about 24 hours','After a month','Never','Before the interview'],'Prompt follow-up.')]],
['Interview day checklist',[
'Before interview day: confirm your appointment time and location, plan your outfit, review your key points and STAR stories and get good sleep. On the day: bring **photo ID**, arrive early, leave all materials outside (no resume copies or notes allowed in the interview) and silence your phone.',
'Remember that interview questions are a **secret topic**—do not discuss them until the event is over.',
"Eat a balanced meal and stay hydrated before the interview. Physical comfort helps you think clearly and stay focused.",
"Plan your travel and timing so you arrive at least 15 minutes early. Rushing increases nerves and can affect your first impression.",
order('Interview day',['Confirm time and location','Dress professionally','Bring photo ID','Arrive early and do a calming routine','Enter with no materials','Greet, interview and close','Keep questions confidential afterward'])]],
['Submission checklist',[
'Before uploading: the **title page** includes all required items; the **personal statement** is no more than two pages, 12-point Arial, double-spaced, 1-inch margins, with a running header and page numbers; the **resume** is one page; both are factual, accurate and error-free; and everything is in **one combined PDF**.',
'Upload by **May 15 at midnight EST**—teams or individuals who do not upload cannot interview. Upload again for ILC if your state used the system.',
"Ask a trusted reviewer to check the final PDF one last time, looking only for formatting and page-order issues.",
"After uploading, download or open the file from the system if possible to confirm the correct version was received.",
sort('Ready to upload?',['Ready','Not ready'],[['Title page lists the opportunity',0],['Statement runs onto a third page',1],['Running header on statement pages',0],['Resume and statement in separate files',1],['Zero spelling errors after two reviews',0]])]],
['Continuing to grow',[
'After the event, reflect: Which answers went well? Which questions surprised you? What would you improve? Use judges’ feedback if available. Job-seeking skills improve with practice—apply for volunteer positions, internships and jobs, and treat each interview as a learning experience.',
'These same skills help with college admissions, scholarships and future health career interviews.',
"Each interview, even a practice one, builds skill. Over time, you will develop a library of polished stories ready for any opportunity.",
"Write brief notes after each practice or real interview about what went well and what to improve. Reviewing these notes speeds your growth.",
match('After the event and next step',[['An answer felt weak','Prepare a stronger STAR story'],['A surprise question','Add it to your practice list'],['Nervous start','Practice your opening more'],['Strong closing','Keep using that structure'],['New experience gained','Update your resume'],['Upcoming application','Tailor your statement']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Closing moments',{a:{text:'At the end, a judge asks, “Is there anything else you’d like us to know?” You say, “No.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What would be stronger?',choices:[['Briefly summarize your fit, express enthusiasm and thank them','b','Correct.',true],['Leave immediately','b','The closing is scored.',false]]},b:{text:'After the interview, a friend competing later asks what questions you were asked. What do you do?',choices:[['Politely decline—questions are a secret topic until the event ends','c','Correct.',true],['Share them','c','Sharing is an ethics violation.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish strong and act with integrity.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['A strong closing (10 points) briefly summarizes your fit, shows genuine enthusiasm, includes a thoughtful question if appropriate and thanks judges, ending with a confident handshake.','Confidence, maturity and enthusiasm (10 points) come from preparation, composure and specific reasons for wanting the role; in real searches, send thank-you notes within about 24 hours.','Use final checklists for the upload (title page, two-page formatted statement, one-page resume, one PDF by May 15) and interview day (photo ID, no materials, early arrival, keep questions secret); reflect and keep improving.'],terms:[['Closing statement','A brief summary of your fit and thanks at the end of an interview.'],['Follow-up','A thank-you note or email after an interview.'],['Maturity','Composed, respectful and responsible conduct.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is the closing worth?','10','5','15','20','Rubric.'),q('What should a strong closing include?','A summary of fit, enthusiasm and thanks','Only “Bye.”','A new topic','Complaints','Closing.'),q('Which shows maturity?','Discussing a mistake honestly and what you learned','Blaming others','Interrupting judges','Arguing','Maturity.'),q('When should a real-world thank-you note be sent?','Within about 24 hours','After a month','Never','Before applying','Follow-up.'),q('What must you bring to the interview?','Photo ID only, no materials','Resume copies','Notecards','A portfolio','Rule.'),q('What happens if you do not upload by May 15?','You cannot interview','You get extra time','You interview anyway','Nothing','Rule.'),q('If a friend asks about the interview questions, you should:','Decline until the event ends','Share them','Post them online','Write them down for others','Ethics.'),q('How many points is confidence, maturity and enthusiasm worth?','10','5','15','20','Rubric.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would close your interview strongly and prepare for both the submission and interview day.',rubric:[['Closing',['summarize','fit','enthusiasm','question','thank','handshake']],['Confidence and maturity',['confidence','maturity','enthusiasm','composed','honest','respectful']],['Checklists',['title page','two pages','one page','one pdf','may 15','photo id','no materials']],['Integrity and growth',['secret topic','confidential','reflect','feedback','update','thank-you note']]],model:'When the judges asked whether I had anything to add, I would close in about 20 seconds: “My CNA training, my experience caring for older adults at the senior center and my reliability at work prepare me well for this role, and I’m excited about Oakwood’s commitment to person-centered care. Thank you for your time today.” Then I would stand, make eye contact and offer each judge a confident handshake. Throughout the interview, I would show confidence, maturity and enthusiasm by speaking calmly, sharing specific reasons I want the position, discussing a past mistake honestly with what I learned and never blaming others. Before submission, I would check that my title page lists the event, my name, division, chapter number, school, chartered association and the CNA position; that my personal statement is no more than two pages in 12-point Arial, double-spaced with 1-inch margins and a running header; that my resume fits on one page; that both are factual and error-free; and that everything is merged into one PDF uploaded before May 15 at midnight EST. On interview day, I would confirm my appointment, wear business attire, bring my photo ID, arrive early, do a breathing routine and enter with no materials. Afterward, I would keep the questions confidential until the event ended, reflect on which answers to strengthen, update my resume with new experiences and, in real applications, send a thank-you note within 24 hours.'},sources:[RULES,OWLLET]};
